{
  "abstract": "Activation readers should preserve what a source expresses, including numerical errors, while assigning details to the requested reference. We study these requirements with three fixed Qwen3-8B Activation Oracle readers. On 128 fresh synthetic accounts, ordinary generation from actual local windows preserves both correct and deliberately incorrect recorded subtotals for every reader; endpoint controls fail. This generator permits a maximum-number shortcut, so a separate 24-identity development test exchanges numerical roles without changing their inventory. All readers recover the exchanged roles under local access. Explicit reference composition remains harder. We freeze a test on 64 new identities, crossing entry order with Current/Earlier references, and compare direct generation, explicit instructions, equal-call direct agreement, and four primitive queries joined without source text or gold values. The original reader's factored output supports 474 of 512 requested answers, with 38 abstentions and no observed contradictions, versus 325 supported, 114 contradicted and 73 abstained under direct agreement. The prespecified gain is 29.10 percentage points, with a paired identity-bootstrap 95% interval of [26.56, 31.64]. Three-layer and GRPO factorization instead support only 175 and 120 answers: invalid intermediate labels sharply reduce useful coverage despite perfect primitive value retrieval. Supporting profile development and complete score replays distinguish source access, requested relevance, arithmetic truth and unsupported currency additions. Decomposition and read-window methods are established prior art; our contribution is the controlled AO comparison, independently confirmed within one generator, and its reader-specific boundary. These expressed-record results neither establish natural-trace generalization nor identify causal use inside the subject.",
